var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/06_interactive/#Interactive-Scattering","page":"Interactive Scattering","text":"One of the standout features of SchroedingerEquation.jl is its ability to render interactive, live-updating simulations using GLMakie.\n\nBecause this generates a graphical window on your local machine, we cannot render the output directly in this documentation. However, we encourage you to copy this code and run it in your local Julia REPL!","title":"Interactive Scattering"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/06_interactive/#Setup","page":"Interactive Scattering","text":"We set up a simple free-space grid with an initial Gaussian wavepacket.\n\nusing SchroedingerEquation\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing GLMakie # GLMakie is required for live_evolution\n\n# Setup\nbasis = RealSpaceGrid1D(-50.0, 50.0, 1000)\npotential = CustomPotential(x -> 0.0)\nham = build_hamiltonian(basis, potential)\n\n# Initial state\nx0 = -20.0\nsigma = 2.0\np0 = 2.0\npsi0_vals = [exp(-(x - x0)^2 / (2 * sigma^2)) * exp(im * p0 * x) for x in basis.x]\npsi0 = Wavefunction1D(ComplexF64.(psi0_vals), basis)\nnormalize!(psi0)","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/06_interactive/#Live-Evolution","page":"Interactive Scattering","text":"By calling live_evolution, a high-performance OpenGL window will pop up. The simulation will run in a separate thread, continuously updating the wavefunction plot in real-time.\n\n# Live interactive visualization\nprintln(\"Starting interactive simulation...\")\nlive_evolution(psi0, ham, 0.02; update_step=5)\n\nTry modifying the potential or the starting momentum p0 to watch how the quantum dynamics change before your eyes!","title":"Live Evolution"},{"category":"section","location":"basis/#Basis-and-Grids","page":"Basis & Grids","text":"SchroedingerEquation.jl uses a real-space discretization for solving the Schrödinger equation. The primary structure for defining the simulation domain is the RealSpaceGrid1D.","title":"Basis and Grids"},{"category":"section","location":"basis/#RealSpaceGrid1D","page":"Basis & Grids","text":"The RealSpaceGrid1D represents a uniform discretization of a 1D spatial interval x_min x_max with N points.","title":"RealSpaceGrid1D"},{"category":"section","location":"basis/#Construction","page":"Basis & Grids","text":"You can construct a grid using physical units from Unitful.jl:\n\nusing SchroedingerEquation\nusing Unitful\n\n# A grid from -10nm to 10nm with 1000 points\nbasis = RealSpaceGrid1D(-10.0u\"nm\", 10.0u\"nm\", 1000)\n\nThe constructor automatically converts the input units to internal atomic units (Bohr) for consistent numerical processing.","title":"Construction"},{"category":"section","location":"basis/#Properties","page":"Basis & Grids","text":"basis.x: A Vector{Float64} containing the coordinates of the grid points.\nbasis.dx: The grid spacing Delta x.\nbasis.N: The number of points.","title":"Properties"},{"category":"section","location":"basis/#Working-with-Wavefunctions","page":"Basis & Grids","text":"Wavefunctions in this package are typically represented by the Wavefunction1D struct, which pairs a complex vector of values with its underlying basis.\n\n# Example: Creating a Gaussian wavepacket on a basis\nx = basis.x\npsi_vals = exp.(-(x .- 0.0).^2 ./ (2 * 1.0^2))\npsi = Wavefunction1D(psi_vals ./ norm(psi_vals), basis)","title":"Working with Wavefunctions"},{"category":"section","location":"basis/#Momentum-Space","page":"Basis & Grids","text":"For solvers like the Split-Step Fourier Method (SSFM), the package automatically handles the transformation between real space and momentum space (k-space).\n\nThe momentum grid is defined such that it is compatible with the Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) of the real-space grid.","title":"Momentum Space"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Solvers","page":"Solvers","text":"SchroedingerEquation.jl provides optimized solvers for both the Time-Independent (TISE) and Time-Dependent (TDSE) Schrödinger Equation.","title":"Solvers"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Time-Independent-(TISE)","page":"Solvers","text":"Solve Hpsi = Epsi to find the stationary states (eigenstates) of the system.\n\n# Find the 5 lowest energy states\nenergies, wavefunctions = solve_tise(ham, 5)\n\nThe TISE solver uses KrylovKit.jl for high-performance iterative diagonalization, making it suitable for very large grids.","title":"Time-Independent (TISE)"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Time-Dependent-(TDSE)","page":"Solvers","text":"Propagate a wavefunction psi(t) according to ihbar fracpartialpartial tpsi = Hpsi.","title":"Time-Dependent (TDSE)"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#1.-Unitary-Crank-Nicolson","page":"Solvers","text":"A stable, 2nd-order implicit method that preserves the norm of the wavefunction. Best for general potentials and non-periodic boundaries.\n\npsi_final = propagate_tdse(psi_init, ham, t_total, dt)","title":"1. Unitary Crank-Nicolson"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#2.-Split-Step-Fourier-Method-(SSFM)","page":"Solvers","text":"An ultra-fast spectral method that uses FFTs to solve the kinetic and potential parts separately. It is O(N log N) and highly accurate for smooth potentials and periodic boundaries.\n\npsi_final = propagate_ssfm(psi_init, pot, t_total, dt)","title":"2. Split-Step Fourier Method (SSFM)"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Performance-Tips","page":"Solvers","text":"","title":"Performance Tips"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Workspaces-for-Parallelism","page":"Solvers","text":"To avoid redundant allocations in loops (e.g., parameter sweeps), use the Workspace Pattern:\n\n# Pre-allocate workspace\nws = SSFMWorkspace(psi_init, pot, dt)\n\n# Use workspace in a loop\nfor i in 1:100\n    propagate_ssfm(psi_init, potentials[i], t_total, dt; workspace=ws)\nend\n\nThis approach is highly recommended for multi-threaded simulations using Threads.@threads.","title":"Workspaces for Parallelism"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"This page contains the auto-generated documentation for the public functions and types in SchroedingerEquation.jl.\n\n","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Core-Types","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Core Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Potentials","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Potentials"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Solvers","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Solvers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Workspaces","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Workspaces"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Utilities","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Utilities"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Visualization","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Visualization"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.RealSpaceGrid1D","page":"API Reference","text":"RealSpaceGrid1D(x_min, x_max, N)\n\nA uniform 1D grid in real space.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.RealSpaceGrid1D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.Wavefunction1D","page":"API Reference","text":"Wavefunction1D(psi, basis)\n\nRepresents a 1D wavefunction on a specific basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.Wavefunction1D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.AbstractBasis","page":"API Reference","text":"AbstractBasis\n\nAbstract supertype for all spatial discretization bases.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.AbstractBasis"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.AbstractPotential","page":"API Reference","text":"AbstractPotential\n\nAbstract supertype for all potential energy functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.AbstractPotential"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.AbstractTimeDependentPotential","page":"API Reference","text":"AbstractTimeDependentPotential\n\nAbstract supertype for potentials that depend on time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.AbstractTimeDependentPotential"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.AbstractHamiltonian","page":"API Reference","text":"AbstractHamiltonian\n\nAbstract supertype for Schrödinger operators.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.AbstractHamiltonian"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.AbstractBoundaryCondition","page":"API Reference","text":"AbstractBoundaryCondition\n\nAbstract supertype for boundary conditions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.AbstractBoundaryCondition"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.PeriodicBoundary","page":"API Reference","text":"PeriodicBoundary()\n\nPeriodic boundary conditions (Born-von Karman).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.PeriodicBoundary"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.HardWallBoundary","page":"API Reference","text":"HardWallBoundary()\n\nHard-wall (Dirichlet) boundary conditions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.HardWallBoundary"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.AbsorbingBoundary","page":"API Reference","text":"AbsorbingBoundary(L, strength=1.0)\n\nComplex Absorbing Potential (CAP) boundary of length L.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.AbsorbingBoundary"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.AbstractWorkspace","page":"API Reference","text":"AbstractWorkspace\n\nAbstract supertype for pre-allocated simulation workspaces.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.AbstractWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.Hamiltonian1D","page":"API Reference","text":"Hamiltonian1D(basis, potential, H_matrix)\n\nRepresents a static 1D Hamiltonian operator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.Hamiltonian1D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.HarmonicPotential","page":"API Reference","text":"HarmonicPotential(omega; m=1.0, x0=0.0)\n\nV(x) = 1/2 * m * omega^2 * (x - x0)^2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.HarmonicPotential"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.SquareWellPotential","page":"API Reference","text":"SquareWellPotential(width, depth; x0=0.0)\n\nV(x) = -depth if |x - x0| < width/2, else 0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.SquareWellPotential"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.OscillatingField","page":"API Reference","text":"OscillatingField(amplitude, frequency)\n\nRepresents a continuous laser field interacting via the dipole approximation: V(x,t) = -x * A * cos(omega * t)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.OscillatingField"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.CombinedPotential","page":"API Reference","text":"CombinedPotential(static_pot, dynamic_pot)\n\nAllows adding a static background potential to a time-dependent one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.CombinedPotential"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.CustomPotential","page":"API Reference","text":"CustomPotential(f)\n\nWraps a Julia function f(x) as a static potential.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.CustomPotential"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.CustomTimeDependentPotential","page":"API Reference","text":"CustomTimeDependentPotential(f)\n\nWraps a Julia function f(x, t) as a time-dependent potential.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.CustomTimeDependentPotential"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.ManolopoulosCAP","page":"API Reference","text":"ManolopoulosCAP\n\nHolds the pre-computed grid profile for the absorbing potential.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.ManolopoulosCAP"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.build_hamiltonian","page":"API Reference","text":"build_hamiltonian(basis, potential, boundary::AbstractBoundaryCondition; hbar=1.0, m=1.0)\n\nCommercial-grade builder that automatically bakes boundary conditions into the static matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.build_hamiltonian"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.solve_tise","page":"API Reference","text":"solve_tise(hamiltonian::Hamiltonian1D, n_states::Int)\n\nSolves the TISE for the lowest n_states energy levels. Returns eigenvalues (energies) and eigenvectors (wavefunctions).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.solve_tise"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.propagate_tdse","page":"API Reference","text":"propagate_tdse(psi0::Wavefunction1D, ham::Hamiltonian1D, t_total, dt; hbar=1.0, save_stride=1, observables=nothing, keep_history=true, workspace=nothing)\n\nPropagate the wavefunction in time for a static Hamiltonian. Uses pre-factorization of the Crank-Nicolson matrix for speed. If a workspace::TDSEWorkspace is provided, it avoids all internal allocations.\n\n\n\n\n\npropagate_tdse(psi0::Wavefunction1D, basis::RealSpaceGrid1D, T_matrix::AbstractMatrix, potential::AbstractTimeDependentPotential, t_total::Float64, dt::Float64; hbar=1.0, save_stride=1, observables=nothing, keep_history=true)\n\nPropagate the wavefunction in time for a time-dependent potential. Evaluates the potential at the midpoint of each timestep. Uses optimized Tridiagonal in-place updates for zero allocation in the loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.propagate_tdse"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.propagate_ssfm","page":"API Reference","text":"propagate_ssfm(psi0::Wavefunction1D, potential::AbstractPotential, t_total, dt; hbar=1.0, m=1.0, save_stride=1, observables=nothing, keep_history=true, workspace=nothing)\n\nPropagate the wavefunction using the Split-Step Fourier Method (SSFM). Extremely fast for smooth potentials and periodic boundaries. O(N log N). If a workspace::SSFMWorkspace is provided, it avoids plan creation and pre-calculation in every call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.propagate_ssfm"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.step_crank_nicolson","page":"API Reference","text":"step_crank_nicolson(psi, H_matrix, dt; hbar=1.0)\n\nPerform one time step using the Crank-Nicolson method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.step_crank_nicolson"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.TDSEWorkspace","page":"API Reference","text":"TDSEWorkspace(A_fact, B, B_psi)\n\nWorkspace for the Crank-Nicolson TDSE solver.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.TDSEWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.SSFMWorkspace","page":"API Reference","text":"SSFMWorkspace(P_plan, P_inv_plan, U_V, U_T)\n\nWorkspace for the Split-Step Fourier Method solver.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.SSFMWorkspace"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.strip_units","page":"API Reference","text":"strip_units(val)\n\nRemoves units from a value, converting to atomic units if it's a Quantity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.strip_units"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.apply_units","page":"API Reference","text":"apply_units(val, unit)\n\nAdds units back to a value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.apply_units"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.probability_density","page":"API Reference","text":"probability_density(wf::Wavefunction1D)\n\nReturns |psi|^2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.probability_density"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.expectation_value","page":"API Reference","text":"expectation_value(wf::Wavefunction1D, operator::AbstractMatrix)\n\nCalculates <psi | O | psi>.\n\n\n\n\n\nexpectation_value(wf::Wavefunction1D, f::Function)\n\nCalculates <psi | f(x) | psi>.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.expectation_value"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.build_momentum_operator","page":"API Reference","text":"build_momentum_operator(basis::RealSpaceGrid1D; hbar=1.0)\n\nConstructs the momentum operator matrix using central finite difference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.build_momentum_operator"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LinearAlgebra.normalize!","page":"API Reference","text":"LinearAlgebra.normalize!(wf::Wavefunction1D)\n\nNormalizes the wavefunction such that integral |psi|^2 dx = 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.normalize!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.animate_evolution","page":"API Reference","text":"animate_evolution(history::Vector{Wavefunction1D}, potential=nothing; filename=\"evolution.mp4\", fps=30, title=\"Wavefunction Evolution\", show_real=true, show_imag=false, show_abs2=true)\n\nGenerates an animation of the wavefunction evolution from a history array. If a potential (AbstractPotential, function, or time-dependent potential) is provided, it's plotted in the background.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.animate_evolution"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SchroedingerEquation.live_evolution","page":"API Reference","text":"live_evolution(...)\n\nInteractive GLMakie visualization. Requires using GLMakie.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SchroedingerEquation.live_evolution"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Theory-and-Numerical-Methods","page":"Theory & Methods","text":"This document details the underlying physical theory and the numerical methods implemented in SchroedingerEquation.jl.","title":"Theory & Numerical Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#1.-Physics-Theory","page":"Theory & Methods","text":"","title":"1. Physics Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Time-Independent-Schrödinger-Equation-(TISE)","page":"Theory & Methods","text":"The stationary states of a quantum system are governed by the Time-Independent Schrödinger Equation:\n\n$\n\n\\hat{H} \\psin(x) = En \\psi_n(x) $\n\nwhere hatH is the Hamiltonian operator:\n\n$\n\n\\hat{H} = -\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m} \\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial x^2} + V(x) $\n\nE_n\n\nis the eigenenergy and psi_n(x) is the eigenfunction corresponding to the n-th state.","title":"Time-Independent Schrödinger Equation (TISE)"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Time-Dependent-Schrödinger-Equation-(TDSE)","page":"Theory & Methods","text":"The time evolution of a quantum state psi(xt) is described by the Time-Dependent Schrödinger Equation:\n\n$\n\ni\\hbar \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t} \\psi(x,t) = \\hat{H}(t) \\psi(x,t) $\n\nIf the Hamiltonian is time-independent, the formal solution is:\n\n$\n\n\\psi(x,t) = \\exp\\left(-\\frac{i \\hat{H} t}{\\hbar}\\right) \\psi(x,0) $","title":"Time-Dependent Schrödinger Equation (TDSE)"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#2.-Unit-System-and-Atomic-Units","page":"Theory & Methods","text":"SchroedingerEquation.jl natively integrates with Unitful.jl and UnitfulAtomic.jl to handle physical quantities. All internal computations are strictly performed in Atomic Units (a.u.) where hbar = 1, m_e = 1, and e = 1.\n\nWhen you provide a parameter with physical units (e.g., 1.0u\"eV/nm^2\" or -10.0u\"nm\"), the package automatically uses UnitfulAtomic.auconvert to strip the units and scale the values down to their dimensionless atomic representations. After the computation, the user can easily convert the dimensionless results (like energy in Hartrees) back into physical units (like eV) if needed.","title":"2. Unit System and Atomic Units"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#3.-Numerical-Methods","page":"Theory & Methods","text":"","title":"3. Numerical Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Discretization-and-the-TISE","page":"Theory & Methods","text":"To numerically solve the TISE, space is discretized into N grid points with a uniform spacing Delta x. The kinetic energy operator is approximated using a standard three-point central finite difference scheme:\n\n$\n\n\\frac{\\partial^2 \\psi}{\\partial x^2} \\approx \\frac{\\psi{i+1} - 2\\psii + \\psi_{i-1}}{\\Delta x^2} $\n\nThis maps the continuous operator hatH to a discrete matrix H. Because the kinetic energy only couples nearest neighbors and the potential energy is purely diagonal, H takes the form of a symmetric tridiagonal matrix. In Julia, we construct this efficiently using LinearAlgebra.SymTridiagonal.\n\nThe lowest k eigenstates are then found with extreme efficiency in O(N) operations using the KrylovKit.eigsolve routine, leveraging the sparse tridiagonal structure.","title":"Discretization and the TISE"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Split-Step-Fourier-Method-(SSFM)-for-TDSE","page":"Theory & Methods","text":"For simulating the TDSE, especially in large domains or long times, the Split-Step Fourier Method (SSFM) is implemented. This method takes advantage of the fact that the kinetic energy operator hatT is diagonal in momentum space, while the potential energy operator hatV is diagonal in position space.\n\nUsing the symmetric Strang splitting, the time evolution operator is approximated as:\n\n$\n\n\\exp\\left(-i \\hat{H} \\Delta t\\right) \\approx \\exp\\left(-i \\frac{\\hat{V} \\Delta t}{2}\\right) \\exp\\left(-i \\hat{T} \\Delta t\\right) \\exp\\left(-i \\frac{\\hat{V} \\Delta t}{2}\\right) + \\mathcal{O}(\\Delta t^3) $\n\nThe algorithm proceeds as follows for each time step Delta t:\n\nApply half a step of the potential operator in position space.\nFourier transform the wavefunction psi xrightarrowmathcalF tildepsi using FFT.\nApply the full kinetic energy operator in momentum space (multiplying by exp(-i frack^22m Delta t)).\nInverse Fourier transform back to position space tildepsi xrightarrowmathcalF^-1 psi.\nApply the second half step of the potential operator.\n\nThis spectral method runs in O(N log N) time, boasts exceptional stability, and inherently imposes periodic boundary conditions.","title":"Split-Step Fourier Method (SSFM) for TDSE"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_free_space/#Free-Space-Dispersion","page":"Free Space Dispersion","text":"This first example demonstrates the absolute basics: how to set up a spatial grid, initialize a Gaussian wavepacket, and propagate it in free space (where the potential V = 0) using the Time-Dependent Schrödinger Equation (TDSE).","title":"Free Space Dispersion"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_free_space/#Step-1:-Initialization","page":"Free Space Dispersion","text":"We begin by importing the necessary packages. SchroedingerEquation handles the physics, LinearAlgebra provides utility for normalization, and CairoMakie is used for high-quality static plotting.\n\nusing SchroedingerEquation\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing CairoMakie\n\n# ==========================================\n# 01. Setup the Spatial Grid\n# ==========================================\n# We define a 1D grid spanning from -50.0 to 50.0 with 1000 discrete points.\nbasis = RealSpaceGrid1D(-50.0, 50.0, 1000)","title":"Step 1: Initialization"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_free_space/#Step-2:-Defining-the-Physics","page":"Free Space Dispersion","text":"Next, we define the environment. Since this is free space, the potential energy is zero everywhere.\n\n# ==========================================\n# 02. Build the Hamiltonian\n# ==========================================\n# Free particle (V = 0)\npotential = CustomPotential(x -> 0.0)\nham = build_hamiltonian(basis, potential)","title":"Step 2: Defining the Physics"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_free_space/#Step-3:-Preparing-the-Initial-State","page":"Free Space Dispersion","text":"We construct a Gaussian wavepacket representing our particle. We give it an initial momentum p0 = 2.0 so that it travels to the right.\n\n# ==========================================\n# 03. Create Initial Wavepacket\n# ==========================================\nx0 = -20.0\nsigma = 2.0\np0 = 2.0\npsi0_vals = [exp(-(x - x0)^2 / (2 * sigma^2)) * exp(im * p0 * x) for x in basis.x]\npsi0 = Wavefunction1D(ComplexF64.(psi0_vals), basis)\nnormalize!(psi0)","title":"Step 3: Preparing the Initial State"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_free_space/#Step-4:-Time-Propagation-and-Visualization","page":"Free Space Dispersion","text":"Finally, we evolve the system forward in time. The propagate_tdse function returns a history array containing the wavefunction at each saved time step.\n\n# ==========================================\n# 04. Time Evolution\n# ==========================================\nt_total = 20.0\ndt = 0.02\nhistory = propagate_tdse(psi0, ham, t_total, dt; save_stride=20)\n\n# ==========================================\n# 05. Visualize\n# ==========================================\nfig = Figure(size=(800, 400))\nax = Axis(fig[1,1], title=\"Free Particle Dispersion\", xlabel=\"x\", ylabel=\"|ψ|²\")\nlines!(ax, basis.x, abs2.(history[1].psi), label=\"t=0\")\nlines!(ax, basis.x, abs2.(history[end].psi), label=\"t=end\")\naxislegend(ax)\n\nsave(\"examples/results/01_free_space.png\", fig)\nprintln(\"Saved results to examples/results/01_free_space.png\")\n\nConsole Output:\n\nSaved results to examples/results/01_free_space.png\n\nResulting Plot:\n\n(Image: Free Particle Dispersion)\n\nAs expected, the wavepacket travels to the right and its width increases (disperses) over time according to the fundamental principles of quantum mechanics!","title":"Step 4: Time Propagation & Visualization"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_absorbing/#Absorbing-Boundaries","page":"Absorbing Boundaries","text":"In open quantum systems, particles can escape the simulated region. Simulating this requires special boundary conditions that absorb the wavepacket without reflecting it back. In this tutorial, we use a Complex Absorbing Potential (CAP).","title":"Absorbing Boundaries"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_absorbing/#Setup","page":"Absorbing Boundaries","text":"We use an AbsorbingBoundary which implements the Manolopoulos CAP, designed specifically for minimizing reflections.\n\nusing SchroedingerEquation\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing CairoMakie\n\n# Setup\nbasis = RealSpaceGrid1D(-20.0, 20.0, 1000) # Higher resolution for smoother CAP\n# Use a slightly larger and stronger CAP for \"0% reflections\"\ncap = AbsorbingBoundary(8.0, 2.0)\nham = build_hamiltonian(basis, CustomPotential(x -> 0.0), cap)","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_absorbing/#Initial-State","page":"Absorbing Boundaries","text":"We start the wavepacket close to the center and give it momentum towards the left boundary.\n\n# Wavepacket\nx0 = -5.0         # Start closer to center to give it space\np0 = 3.0          # Momentum\ndirection = -1.0  # Moving to the left towards the CAP\n\npsi0_vals = exp.(-(basis.x .- x0).^2 ./ 2.0) .* exp.(im * p0 * direction .* basis.x)\npsi0 = Wavefunction1D(ComplexF64.(psi0_vals), basis)\nnormalize!(psi0)","title":"Initial State"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_absorbing/#Tracking-Survival-Probability","page":"Absorbing Boundaries","text":"Because the boundary absorbs the wave, the total norm of the wavefunction will decrease over time. We track this using the observables feature.\n\n# Track norm\nobs = (norm = wf -> sum(abs2, wf.psi) * wf.basis.dx,)\nhistory, obs_data = propagate_tdse(psi0, ham, 20.0, 0.05; observables=obs)\n\n# Visualization\nfig = Figure(size=(800, 400))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], title=\"Survival Probability (CAP Absorption)\", xlabel=\"Time Step\", ylabel=\"Norm\")\nlines!(ax, obs_data.norm, color=:red, linewidth=2.5)\nsave(\"examples/results/04_absorbing_boundaries.png\", fig)\n\n# Animate without passing the boundary object as a potential\nanimate_evolution(history; filename=\"examples/results/04_absorbing.mp4\", fps=30, title=\"Absorption Dynamics\")\nprintln(\"Saved results to examples/results/04_absorbing_boundaries.png and .mp4\")\n\nConsole Output:\n\nSaved results to examples/results/04_absorbing_boundaries.png and .mp4\n\nResulting Plot:\n\n(Image: Survival Probability)\n\nAnimation:\n\n(Image: Absorption Dynamics)","title":"Tracking Survival Probability"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/03_tunneling/#Quantum-Tunneling","page":"Quantum Tunneling","text":"In this example, we tackle a phenomenon that is impossible in classical mechanics: Quantum Tunneling. We will fire a wavepacket at a rectangular potential barrier. The kinetic energy of the wavepacket is strictly lower than the barrier's potential height. Classically, it should completely bounce off. Let's see what happens quantum mechanically.","title":"Quantum Tunneling"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/03_tunneling/#Setup-and-Initialization","page":"Quantum Tunneling","text":"We use the built-in SquareWellPotential to construct a barrier. Notice that by giving it a negative depth (e.g., -15.0), it acts as a positive barrier in our Hamiltonian conventions.\n\nusing SchroedingerEquation\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing CairoMakie\n\n# Setup\nbasis = RealSpaceGrid1D(-50.0, 50.0, 1000)\n\n# A barrier in the center (positive potential height 15)\nbarrier = SquareWellPotential(2.0, -15.0; x0=0.0) \nham = build_hamiltonian(basis, barrier)","title":"Setup and Initialization"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/03_tunneling/#The-Tunneling-Regime","page":"Quantum Tunneling","text":"We carefully set the momentum p0 = 5.0. In our natural units (m=1 hbar=1), the kinetic energy is E = p^2  2m = 125. Since 125  150, we are firmly in the tunneling regime.\n\n# Initial state: A moving Gaussian wavepacket starting on the left\nx0 = -25.0\nsigma = 2.0\np0 = 5.0 \npsi0_vals = [exp(-(x - x0)^2 / (2 * sigma^2)) * exp(im * p0 * x) for x in basis.x]\npsi0 = Wavefunction1D(ComplexF64.(psi0_vals), basis)\nnormalize!(psi0)\n\n# TDSE: Tunneling animation\nt_total = 12.0\ndt = 0.02\nhistory = propagate_tdse(psi0, ham, t_total, dt; save_stride=5)","title":"The Tunneling Regime"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/03_tunneling/#Rendering-the-Animation","page":"Quantum Tunneling","text":"Instead of plotting static lines, we use the incredibly powerful animate_evolution tool to generate an MP4 video of the entire process!\n\n# Visualization\nanimate_evolution(history, barrier; filename=\"examples/results/03_tunneling.mp4\", fps=30, title=\"Tunneling Effect\")\nprintln(\"Saved animation to examples/results/03_tunneling.mp4\")\n\nConsole Output:\n\nSaved animation to examples/results/03_tunneling.mp4\n\nResulting Animation:\n\n(Image: Quantum Tunneling)\n\nNotice how the majority of the wave is reflected, but a small fraction of the wavepacket successfully emerges and continues propagating on the right side of the barrier!","title":"Rendering the Animation"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_excitation/#Ultrafast-Excitation","page":"Ultrafast Excitation","text":"Simulating time-dependent potentials (like a laser pulse hitting an electron in a trap) is a core capability of SchroedingerEquation.jl.\n\nIn this tutorial, we place an electron in the ground state of a harmonic oscillator and then strike it with an ultrashort, oscillating laser pulse.","title":"Ultrafast Excitation"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_excitation/#Defining-the-Time-Dependent-Potential","page":"Ultrafast Excitation","text":"We use CustomTimeDependentPotential, which accepts a function of both position x and time t. Here, we model a Gaussian-enveloped laser pulse V(x t) = -x cdot E(t) cdot cos(omega t).\n\nusing SchroedingerEquation\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing SparseArrays\nusing CairoMakie\n\n# Setup\nbasis = RealSpaceGrid1D(-30.0, 30.0, 1000)\nstatic_pot = HarmonicPotential(1.0)\nboundary = AbsorbingBoundary(5.0)\n\n# Laser pulse: enveloped oscillating field\npulse = CustomTimeDependentPotential((x, t) -> -x * 0.1 * exp(-((t - 5.0)/1.0)^2) * cos(1.0 * t))","title":"Defining the Time-Dependent Potential"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_excitation/#Initializing-the-Ground-State","page":"Ultrafast Excitation","text":"To calculate the initial state, we build the static Hamiltonian (without the laser) and solve the Time-Independent Schrödinger Equation (TISE) for the ground state.\n\n# Ground state\nham_static = build_hamiltonian(basis, static_pot, boundary)\nenergies, wavefunctions = solve_tise(ham_static, 1)\npsi0 = wavefunctions[1]","title":"Initializing the Ground State"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_excitation/#Dynamic-Propagation","page":"Ultrafast Excitation","text":"We pass the static ham_static.H_matrix and the time-dependent pulse to propagate_tdse. The solver is smart enough to efficiently add the time-dependent components to the diagonal of the matrix at each time step. We also track the Energy expectation value.\n\n# Dynamic propagation\nt_total = 12.0\ndt = 0.05\n\n# Track normalized energy expectation value\nobs = (E = wf -> real(expectation_value(wf, ham_static.H_matrix)) / (sum(abs2, wf.psi) * wf.basis.dx),)\nhistory, obs_data = propagate_tdse(psi0, basis, ham_static.H_matrix, pulse, t_total, dt; observables=obs)\n\n# Visualization\nfig = Figure(size=(800, 400))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], title=\"Energy Expectation Value <E(t)>\", xlabel=\"Time Step\", ylabel=\"Energy\")\nlines!(ax, real.(obs_data.E), color=:purple, linewidth=2.5)\n\nsave(\"examples/results/05_excitation.png\", fig)\nprintln(\"Saved results to examples/results/05_excitation.png\")\n\nConsole Output:\n\nSaved results to examples/results/05_excitation.png\n\nResulting Plot:\n\n(Image: Ultrafast Excitation)\n\nNotice how the energy spikes precisely when the laser pulse peaks at t = 50, meaning the electron has been successfully excited into higher energy states!","title":"Dynamic Propagation"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_observables/#Phase-and-Observables","page":"Phase and Observables","text":"In this tutorial, we will explore how to track quantum observables (like position langle x rangle and momentum langle p rangle) over time while a wavepacket evolves in a potential trap.","title":"Phase and Observables"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_observables/#Setup-and-Initialization","page":"Phase and Observables","text":"We set up a harmonic potential V(x) = frac12 k x^2 and initialize our wavepacket as a coherent state (a Gaussian displaced from the center). \n\nusing SchroedingerEquation\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing CairoMakie\n\n# Setup\nbasis = RealSpaceGrid1D(-20.0, 20.0, 500)\npotential = HarmonicPotential(1.0)\nham = build_hamiltonian(basis, potential)\n\n# Initial state (coherent state/displaced gaussian)\npsi0_vals = exp.(-(basis.x .+ 5.0).^2 ./ 2.0)\npsi0 = Wavefunction1D(ComplexF64.(psi0_vals), basis)\nnormalize!(psi0)","title":"Setup and Initialization"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_observables/#Tracking-Observables","page":"Phase and Observables","text":"The propagate_tdse function accepts an optional observables named tuple. We define functions that compute the expectation values. For momentum, we use the build_momentum_operator utility.\n\n# Track position and momentum\nobs = (x = wf -> expectation_value(wf, x -> x), \n       p = wf -> expectation_value(wf, build_momentum_operator(basis)))\n\nt_total = 10.0\ndt = 0.05\nhistory, obs_data = propagate_tdse(psi0, ham, t_total, dt; observables=obs)","title":"Tracking Observables"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_observables/#Visualization","page":"Phase and Observables","text":"Finally, we plot the oscillating position and momentum, demonstrating the classic harmonic oscillator behavior! We also plot the final wavefunction components.\n\n# Visualization\nfig = Figure(size=(800, 600))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], title=\"Observables over time\", xlabel=\"Time Step\")\nlines!(ax1, float.(obs_data.x), label=\"<x>\")\nlines!(ax1, real.(obs_data.p), label=\"<p>\")\naxislegend(ax1)\n\nax2 = Axis(fig[2, 1], title=\"Wavefunction components (Final state)\", xlabel=\"x\")\nwf_final = history[end]\n# Normalize potential for plotting\nV_vals = [ham.potential(xi) for xi in basis.x]\nV_plot = real.(V_vals) ./ maximum(abs.(V_vals)) .* maximum(abs2.(wf_final.psi))\nlines!(ax2, basis.x, V_plot, color=:black, linestyle=:dash, label=\"V(x) (scaled)\")\nlines!(ax2, basis.x, real.(wf_final.psi), label=\"Re(ψ)\")\nlines!(ax2, basis.x, real.(imag.(wf_final.psi)), label=\"Im(ψ)\")\nlines!(ax2, basis.x, real.(abs2.(wf_final.psi)), label=\"|ψ|²\")\naxislegend(ax2)\n\nsave(\"examples/results/02_observables.png\", fig)\nprintln(\"Saved results to examples/results/02_observables.png\")\n\nConsole Output:\n\nSaved results to examples/results/02_observables.png\n\nResulting Plot:\n\n(Image: Observables over time)","title":"Visualization"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/08_distributed/#Distributed-Ensemble","page":"Distributed Ensemble","text":"For massive scale simulations (like running on a supercomputing cluster), standard multi-threading isn't enough. SchroedingerEquation.jl works seamlessly with Julia's Distributed standard library, allowing you to run ensemble simulations across multiple processes and even multiple machines over a network.","title":"Distributed Ensemble"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/08_distributed/#Adding-Workers","page":"Distributed Ensemble","text":"We begin by adding worker processes.\n\nusing Distributed\n\n# 1. Add Workers (e.g., 4 processes)\nif nprocs() == 1\n    addprocs(4)\nend","title":"Adding Workers"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/08_distributed/#Loading-Dependencies","page":"Distributed Ensemble","text":"Because the workers are entirely separate memory processes, we must load our dependencies @everywhere.\n\n# 2. Load Package on all workers\n@everywhere begin\n    using Pkg\n    Pkg.activate(\".\")\n    using SchroedingerEquation\n    using Unitful\n    using LinearAlgebra\nend\n\nprintln(\"Running distributed ensemble on $(nprocs()) processes...\")","title":"Loading Dependencies"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/08_distributed/#The-Distributed-Map","page":"Distributed Ensemble","text":"We want to calculate the final mean displacement of a wavepacket as a function of its initial momentum. \n\nWe use the incredibly powerful pmap (Parallel Map) function. pmap automatically load-balances the workload, sending each iteration of the loop to whatever worker process is currently free!\n\n# 3. Setup Simulation Parameters\nbasis = RealSpaceGrid1D(-50.0u\"nm\", 50.0u\"nm\", 1000)\npotential = CustomPotential(x -> 0.0) # Free space\np0_list = range(1.0, 5.0, length=20)u\"hbar/nm\"\n\n# 4. Distributed Loop\nresults = pmap(p0_list) do p0\n    x0 = -20.0u\"nm\"\n    sigma = 2.0u\"nm\"\n    psi_vals = [exp(-(x - strip_units(x0))^2 / (2 * strip_units(sigma)^2)) * exp(im * strip_units(p0) * x) for x in basis.x]\n    psi0 = Wavefunction1D(ComplexF64.(psi_vals), basis)\n    normalize!(psi0)\n    \n    t_total = 50.0u\"fs\"\n    dt = 0.1u\"fs\"\n    \n    # Run simulation\n    history = propagate_ssfm(psi0, potential, t_total, dt; keep_history=true)\n    \n    # Calculate final mean position <x>\n    final_wf = history[end]\n    mean_x = expectation_value(final_wf, x -> x)\n    return mean_x\nend","title":"The Distributed Map"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/08_distributed/#Visualization","page":"Distributed Ensemble","text":"# 5. Process and Visualize\nprintln(\"All workers finished. Generating ensemble plot...\")\n\nusing CairoMakie\nfig = Figure(size=(800, 600))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], title=\"Ensemble Wavepacket Displacement\",\n          xlabel=\"Initial Momentum (hbar/nm)\", ylabel=\"Final Position <x> (nm)\")\n\nscatter!(ax, ustrip.(p0_list), results, color=:teal, markersize=15)\nlines!(ax, ustrip.(p0_list), results, color=:teal, linestyle=:dash)\n\nsave(\"examples/results/08_distributed_ensemble.png\", fig)\nprintln(\"Ensemble complete. Results saved to examples/results/08_distributed_ensemble.png\")\n\nConsole Output:\n\nRunning distributed ensemble on 5 processes...\nAll workers finished. Generating ensemble plot...\nEnsemble complete. Results saved to examples/results/08_distributed_ensemble.png\n\nResulting Plot:\n\n(Image: Distributed Ensemble)","title":"Visualization"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/07_parallel/#Parallel-Parameter-Sweep","page":"Parallel Parameter Sweep","text":"In quantum mechanics, we often need to calculate probabilities over a range of parameters (like computing the transmission probability of a particle across different incident energies). \n\nBecause these simulations are completely independent, SchroedingerEquation.jl natively supports multi-threading out-of-the-box using Julia's @threads macro!","title":"Parallel Parameter Sweep"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/07_parallel/#Setup","page":"Parallel Parameter Sweep","text":"We set up a barrier with a width of 2.0 nm and a height of 0.5 eV. We will sweep over incident particle energies from 0.1 eV to 1.0 eV.\n\nusing SchroedingerEquation\nusing Unitful\nusing Base.Threads\nusing LinearAlgebra\n\n# 1. Setup Common Basis\nbasis = RealSpaceGrid1D(-50.0u\"nm\", 50.0u\"nm\", 2000)\n\n# 2. Define the Barrier\nbarrier_width = 2.0u\"nm\"\nbarrier_height = 0.5u\"eV\"\npotential = SquareWellPotential(barrier_width, -barrier_height; x0=0.0u\"nm\")\n\n# 3. Parameter Sweep: Incident Energies\nenergies = range(0.1, 1.0, length=24)u\"eV\"\nresults = zeros(length(energies))\n\nprintln(\"Starting parallel sweep on \", nthreads(), \" threads...\")","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/07_parallel/#The-Multi-threaded-Loop","page":"Parallel Parameter Sweep","text":"By wrapping our for loop in @threads, Julia automatically distributes the workload across the available CPU cores.\n\nInside the loop, we use the SSFMWorkspace to pre-allocate memory and call the ultra-fast propagate_ssfm solver. Because each thread uses its own local workspace, this approach is perfectly thread-safe and extremely fast.\n\n@threads for i in 1:length(energies)\n    E = energies[i]\n    \n    # Setup initial wavepacket with energy E\n    m = 1.0u\"me\"\n    hbar = 1.0u\"hbar\"\n    p0 = sqrt(2 * m * E)\n    \n    x0 = -20.0u\"nm\"\n    sigma = 2.0u\"nm\"\n    psi_vals = [exp(-(x - strip_units(x0))^2 / (2 * strip_units(sigma)^2)) * exp(im * strip_units(p0) * x) for x in basis.x]\n    psi0 = Wavefunction1D(ComplexF64.(psi_vals), basis)\n    normalize!(psi0)\n    \n    dt = 0.05u\"fs\"\n    t_total = 100.0u\"fs\"\n    \n    # We create a local workspace for this thread\n    ws = SSFMWorkspace(psi0, potential, dt; m=m, hbar=hbar)\n    \n    # Propagate (don't keep history to save memory)\n    history = propagate_ssfm(psi0, potential, t_total, dt; \n                             workspace=ws, m=m, hbar=hbar, keep_history=true)\n    \n    # Calculate Transmission: Fraction of norm on the right side (x > 5.0nm)\n    final_psi = history[end].psi\n    transmission = sum(abs2, final_psi[basis.x .> strip_units(5.0u\"nm\")]) * basis.dx\n    results[i] = transmission\n    \n    println(\"Thread $(threadid()): Finished E = $E, T = $(round(transmission, digits=3))\")\nend","title":"The Multi-threaded Loop"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/07_parallel/#Visualization","page":"Parallel Parameter Sweep","text":"Once all threads have finished, we plot the transmission probabilities.\n\n# 4. Visualization of Results\nusing CairoMakie\nfig = Figure(size=(800, 600))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], title=\"Tunneling Probability vs Energy\",\n          xlabel=\"Energy (eV)\", ylabel=\"Transmission Coefficient\")\n\nlines!(ax, ustrip.(energies), results, color=:blue, linewidth=3)\nscatter!(ax, ustrip.(energies), results, color=:red)\n\n# Add barrier height indicator\nvlines!(ax, [ustrip(barrier_height)], color=:black, linestyle=:dash, label=\"Barrier Height\")\n\nsave(\"examples/results/07_tunneling_sweep.png\", fig)\nprintln(\"Sweep complete. Results saved to examples/results/07_tunneling_sweep.png\")\n\nConsole Output:\n\nStarting parallel sweep on 1 threads...\nThread 1: Finished E = 0.1 eV, T = 0.0\nThread 1: Finished E = 0.1391304347826087 eV, T = 0.0\n...\nThread 1: Finished E = 0.9608695652173913 eV, T = 0.0\nThread 1: Finished E = 1.0 eV, T = 0.0\nSweep complete. Results saved to examples/results/07_tunneling_sweep.png\n\n(Note: The example above was run with a single thread locally, but to utilize multi-threading, simply start julia with julia -t auto!)\n\nResulting Plot:\n\n(Image: Parallel Sweep)","title":"Visualization"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Tutorials-and-Examples","page":"Tutorials & Examples","text":"Explore common physics problems solved with SchroedingerEquation.jl.","title":"Tutorials & Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#1.-Free-Space-Dispersion","page":"Tutorials & Examples","text":"This example demonstrates how to set up the spatial grid, initialize a Gaussian wavepacket, and propagate it in free space (V = 0) using the Time-Dependent Schrödinger Equation (TDSE). \n\nusing SchroedingerEquation\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing CairoMakie\n\n# ==========================================\n# 01. Setup the Spatial Grid\n# ==========================================\nbasis = RealSpaceGrid1D(-50.0, 50.0, 1000)\n\n# ==========================================\n# 02. Build the Hamiltonian\n# ==========================================\n# Free particle (V = 0)\npotential = CustomPotential(x -> 0.0)\nham = build_hamiltonian(basis, potential)\n\n# ==========================================\n# 03. Create Initial Wavepacket\n# ==========================================\nx0 = -20.0\nsigma = 2.0\np0 = 2.0\npsi0_vals = [exp(-(x - x0)^2 / (2 * sigma^2)) * exp(im * p0 * x) for x in basis.x]\npsi0 = Wavefunction1D(ComplexF64.(psi0_vals), basis)\nnormalize!(psi0)\n\n# ==========================================\n# 04. Time Evolution\n# ==========================================\nt_total = 20.0\ndt = 0.02\nhistory = propagate_tdse(psi0, ham, t_total, dt; save_stride=20)\n\n# ==========================================\n# 05. Visualize\n# ==========================================\nfig = Figure(size=(800, 400))\nax = Axis(fig[1,1], title=\"Free Particle Dispersion\", xlabel=\"x\", ylabel=\"|ψ|²\")\nlines!(ax, basis.x, abs2.(history[1].psi), label=\"t=0\")\nlines!(ax, basis.x, abs2.(history[end].psi), label=\"t=end\")\naxislegend(ax)\n\nOutput:\n\n(Image: Free Particle Dispersion)\n\nAs expected, the wavepacket travels to the right and its width increases (disperses) over time!\n\n","title":"1. Free Space Dispersion"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#2.-Quantum-Tunneling","page":"Tutorials & Examples","text":"In this example, we fire a wavepacket at a rectangular potential barrier. The energy of the wavepacket is lower than the barrier height, so classically it should bounce off. However, quantum mechanics allows it to tunnel through.\n\nusing SchroedingerEquation\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing CairoMakie\n\n# Setup\nbasis = RealSpaceGrid1D(-50.0, 50.0, 1000)\n\n# A barrier in the center (positive potential height 15)\nbarrier = SquareWellPotential(2.0, -15.0; x0=0.0) \nham = build_hamiltonian(basis, barrier)\n\n# Initial state: A moving Gaussian wavepacket starting on the left\nx0 = -25.0\nsigma = 2.0\np0 = 5.0 # E = p^2/2 = 12.5 < 15.0 (Barrier height) -> Tunneling regime\npsi0_vals = [exp(-(x - x0)^2 / (2 * sigma^2)) * exp(im * p0 * x) for x in basis.x]\npsi0 = Wavefunction1D(ComplexF64.(psi0_vals), basis)\nnormalize!(psi0)\n\n# TDSE: Tunneling animation\nt_total = 12.0\ndt = 0.02\nhistory = propagate_tdse(psi0, ham, t_total, dt; save_stride=5)\n\n# Visualization\nanimate_evolution(history, barrier; filename=\"03_tunneling.mp4\", fps=30, title=\"Tunneling Effect\")\n\nOutput:\n\n(Image: Quantum Tunneling)\n\nNotice how a small fraction of the wavepacket successfully emerges on the right side of the barrier!\n\n\n\nFor more advanced examples, such as multi-threading, python integration, or complex absorbing potentials, check out the examples/ directory in the repository!","title":"2. Quantum Tunneling"},{"category":"section","location":"hamiltonian/#Potentials-and-Hamiltonians","page":"Hamiltonians","text":"The physics of your simulation is defined by the potential energy operator V(x) and the resulting Hamiltonian H = T + V.","title":"Potentials and Hamiltonians"},{"category":"section","location":"hamiltonian/#Potentials","page":"Hamiltonians","text":"SchroedingerEquation.jl provides several built-in potential types, all of which support physical units.","title":"Potentials"},{"category":"section","location":"hamiltonian/#Built-in-Potentials","page":"Hamiltonians","text":"HarmonicPotential(k): V(x) = frac12 k x^2.\nSquareWellPotential(V0, width): A finite square well of depth V0 and given width.\nCustomPotential(f): Define any arbitrary potential using a Julia function f(x).","title":"Built-in Potentials"},{"category":"section","location":"hamiltonian/#Example:-Custom-Potential","page":"Hamiltonians","text":"using SchroedingerEquation\nusing Unitful\n\n# Define a double well potential\nV_func(x) = (x^2 - 5.0^2)^2 * 0.01\npot = CustomPotential(V_func)","title":"Example: Custom Potential"},{"category":"section","location":"hamiltonian/#Building-the-Hamiltonian","page":"Hamiltonians","text":"The build_hamiltonian function assembles the kinetic and potential energy operators into a single object.\n\nham = build_hamiltonian(basis, pot)","title":"Building the Hamiltonian"},{"category":"section","location":"hamiltonian/#Numerical-Implementation","page":"Hamiltonians","text":"For the Time-Independent Schrödinger Equation (TISE), the Hamiltonian is represented as a Sparse Matrix (specifically a SymTridiagonal or SparseMatrixCSC). This allows for O(N) memory scaling and very fast eigen-solving.","title":"Numerical Implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"hamiltonian/#Boundary-Conditions","page":"Hamiltonians","text":"You can specify boundary conditions when building the Hamiltonian or setting up solvers:\n\nHardWallBoundary(): The wavefunction vanishes at the grid edges (Dirichlet).\nPeriodicBoundary(): The system is treated as periodic (Born-von Karman).\nAbsorbingBoundary(L): Adds a Complex Absorbing Potential (CAP) of length L to the edges to simulate open boundaries (scattering).\n\n# Hamiltonian with Absorbing Boundaries\nham = build_hamiltonian(basis, pot; boundary=AbsorbingBoundary(2.0u\"nm\"))","title":"Boundary Conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Python-Support","page":"Python Support","text":"SchroedingerEquation.jl provides a high-performance Python interface using juliacall. This allows you to leverage the speed of Julia's Schrödinger solvers directly from within your Python research environment.","title":"Python Support"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Installation","page":"Python Support","text":"To use the Python interface, you can install the package directly from the repository:\n\npip install \"git+https://github.com/physarief78/SchroedingerEquation.jl.git#egg=schroedingerequation&subdirectory=python\"\n\nAlternatively, if you have the repository cloned locally:\n\ncd python\npip install .","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Quick-Start","page":"Python Support","text":"The Python API is designed to feel native to Python users, with automatic conversion to and from NumPy arrays.\n\nimport schroedingerequation as se\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\n# 1. Setup a grid with physical units\nbasis = se.RealSpaceGrid1D(se.unit(\"-2.0u\\\"nm\\\"\"), se.unit(\"2.0u\\\"nm\\\"\"), 500)\n\n# 2. Define a potential (e.g., Harmonic Oscillator)\npot = se.HarmonicPotential(se.unit(\"500.0u\\\"eV/nm^2\\\"\"))\n\n# 3. Solve the Time-Independent Schrödinger Equation (TISE)\nham = se.build_hamiltonian(basis, pot)\nenergies, psi = se.solve_tise(ham, 3)\n\n# 4. Access results as NumPy arrays\nx = np.array(basis.x)\nplt.plot(x, psi[:, 0]) # Plot the ground state\nplt.show()","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Features","page":"Python Support","text":"","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Unit-Handling","page":"Python Support","text":"Physical units are handled via the se.unit() helper, which supports both standard physical units (via Unitful.jl) and atomic units (via UnitfulAtomic.jl).","title":"Unit Handling"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#NumPy-Integration","page":"Python Support","text":"Grid coordinates: np.array(basis.x)\nEigenenergies: Returned as a standard 1D NumPy array.\nWavefunctions: Returned as a 2D NumPy array with shape (n_points, n_states).","title":"NumPy Integration"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Demo-Example","page":"Python Support","text":"A complete, professional example with high-resolution plotting is available in the repository at examples/python_demo/src/main.py.","title":"Demo Example"},{"category":"section","location":"#SchroedingerEquation.jl","page":"Home","text":"Welcome to the documentation for SchroedingerEquation.jl, a high-performance, rigorous, and unit-aware solver for the 1D Schrödinger equation. \n\nThis package was born out of the need for a simulation tool that bridges the gap between educational toy models and hardcore research applications. It is designed for researchers, computational physicists, and students who need robust simulation tools for quantum mechanics, ranging from calculating simple bound states to simulating ultrafast time-dependent laser excitations.\n\nBy leveraging the Julia language's speed and ecosystem, SchroedingerEquation.jl allows you to define complex potentials and wavepackets and instantly solve for their eigenstates or propagate them through time without sacrificing performance.","title":"SchroedingerEquation.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Core-Features","page":"Home","text":"Strict Unit Support: Native integration with Unitful.jl and UnitfulAtomic.jl ensures you never drop a factor of hbar, miscalculate a mass, or get your electron-volts mixed up with your Joules.\nFast Solvers:\nTISE (Time-Independent): Extremely fast O(N) Eigen-solvers using SymTridiagonal matrices and KrylovKit. Calculate hundreds of bound states in milliseconds.\nTDSE (Time-Dependent): Stable Unitary Crank-Nicolson algorithms and ultra-fast Split-Step Fourier Methods (SSFM) for seamlessly propagating wavepackets through time.\nAdvanced Boundaries: Full support for Periodic boundaries, Hard Walls, and Manolopoulos Complex Absorbing Potentials (CAP) for accurately simulating open quantum systems (like electrons escaping a detector).\nVisualization: Beautiful interactive live feedback and professional GIF/MP4 animations seamlessly integrated via Makie.jl and CairoMakie.","title":"Core Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#Theory-and-Methods","page":"Home","text":"For a deeper dive into the physics, unit conversions, and mathematical formulation of the numerical methods (like Finite Difference and SSFM) powering the solver, please refer to the Theory & Methods section in the Manual.","title":"Theory & Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"#Getting-Started","page":"Home","text":"Let's look at a very simple example: solving for the ground state energy of an electron in a harmonic trap. Notice how we use physical units explicitly!\n\nusing SchroedingerEquation\nusing Unitful\nusing UnitfulAtomic\n\n# 1. Define a 20nm spatial grid with 1000 points\nbasis = RealSpaceGrid1D(-10.0u\"nm\", 10.0u\"nm\", 1000)\n\n# 2. Define a harmonic potential trap (k = 1.0 eV/nm²)\npot = HarmonicPotential(1.0u\"eV/nm^2\")\n\n# 3. Build the Hamiltonian\nham = build_hamiltonian(basis, pot)\n\n# 4. Solve the Time-Independent Schrödinger Equation for the lowest 3 states\nenergies, states = solve_tise(ham, 3)\n\n# 5. Extract and print the ground state energy in eV\nprintln(\"Ground state energy: \", apply_units(energies[1], u\"eV\"))\n\nOutput:\n\nGround state energy: 4.532029953521174e-5 eV","title":"Getting Started"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"Because the package is currently pending registration in the Julia General Registry, you must install it directly from the GitHub repository URL using the Julia package manager:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/physarief78/SchroedingerEquation.jl\")","title":"Installation"}]
}
